  "reveal": {
    "afterSuccess": {
      "data": "Tabulate is a faithful bundle of Charter ∘ Parser, so it produced exactly the clear chart Reporter expects — and the report came out. Bundling the OTHER two (Reporter ∘ Charter) and running it after Parser produces the very same report. Grouping is free: (h ∘ g) ∘ f = h ∘ (g ∘ f).",
      "alchemy": "Forge faithfully bundles Toolsmith ∘ Smelter, producing what Caster expects. Bundling the other two instead reaches the very same alloy. (h ∘ g) ∘ f = h ∘ (g ∘ f).",
      "spellcraft": "Conjure faithfully bundles Kindler ∘ Ignition, producing what Warder expects. Bundling the other two instead reaches the very same ward. (h ∘ g) ∘ f = h ∘ (g ∘ f).",
      "abstract": "The faithful composite fed h the right value. Either grouping yields the same result: (h ∘ g) ∘ f = h ∘ (g ∘ f)."
    },
    "summary": "Associativity: how you group a chain doesn't change the result. (h ∘ g) ∘ f = h ∘ (g ∘ f) — provided each bundle genuinely is the composite it claims to be.",
    "notation": ["(h ∘ g) ∘ f = h ∘ (g ∘ f)", "h ∘ g ∘ f"],
    "moreFormal": "Composition is associative, so chains need no parentheses: h ∘ g ∘ f is unambiguous. With the identity law, this is what makes a category.",
    "programmerAnalogy": "compose(compose(h, g), f) === compose(h, compose(g, f))."
  },
  "glossaryUnlocks": ["associativity"],
